# fine-tuned foundation-model forecaster checkpoints — regenerable from the
# committed seed-0 finetune scripts; too large for the free LFS quota
results/Heston/preprocessing_with_log_returns/forecaster/weights/*.pt
# TimeDiT 1M path-shadowing scenario bank — kept on disk only, NOT pushed
# (Theo's instruction). Regenerable via path_shadowing/gen_banks.py --variant raw;
# too large for the free LFS quota. Only ONE bank exists: TimeDiT's PS runs on the
# no-preproc checkpoint (preprocessing degrades it), so the bank lives under
# baseline_no_preproc/. The top-level path stays ignored as a safety net.
results/Heston/preprocessing_with_log_returns/TimeDiT/path_shadowing/bank/*.npy
results/Heston/preprocessing_with_log_returns/TimeDiT/baseline_no_preproc/path_shadowing/bank/*.npy
# TimeDiT model checkpoints — kept ON DISK in place (weights/seed_N_model.pt) so
# they stay accessible any time, but NOT pushed (Theo's instruction, 2026-07-31:
# "save the checkpoints inside the disk and do not push them ... they just do not
# appear in the github"). DiT-S is 32,512,897 fp32 params = 124.1 MiB per file,
# over GitHub's 100 MiB per-file HARD cap, and 7 files = 869 MiB would exhaust the
# free LFS tier (1 GiB) in a single push — see M10/M12.
# Regenerable: code/train_timedit_logret.py --seed N (deterministic).
# The small seed_N_config.json recipe files stay tracked.
results/Heston/preprocessing_with_log_returns/TimeDiT/weights/*.pt
results/Heston/preprocessing_with_log_returns/TimeDiT/baseline_no_preproc/weights/*.pt
# Experiment B regime oracle — ExtraTrees(500 trees, 77 features) serialises to
# 529 MiB, over GitHub's 100 MiB per-file HARD cap. The reproducible artefact is
# the gate accuracy in oracle/gate_report.json (tracked); the blob is refit locally
# via protocol/experiments/scripts/fit_heston_mixture_oracle.py --workers 16.
dataset/Heston/new_experiments/experiment_B/oracle/oracle.joblib

# new_experiments TimeDiT model checkpoints — same model, same instruction, same cap as
# the preprocessing_with_log_returns block above. DiT-S at 32,463,745 fp32 params
# serialises to 130,100,902 bytes = 124.1 MiB per file; 10 files (2 experiments x 5 seeds)
# = 1.21 GiB, every one of them over GitHub's 100 MiB per-file HARD cap, and LFS was ruled
# out on 2026-07-30 ("add no new ones").
# Theo, 2026-07-31: "save the checkpoints inside the disk and do not push them ... they
# just do not appear in the github". They REMAIN ON DISK, so the READMEs' evidence of five
# independent retrainings (5 distinct MD5s per experiment) stays verifiable locally with:
#   md5sum weights/seed_*_model.pt | sort -u | wc -l   ->  5
# Regenerable: code/train_timedit_experiment.py --seed N (deterministic).
# The small seed_N_config.json recipe files stay tracked, as for the other methods.
results/new_experiments/experiment_A/TimeDiT/weights/*.pt
results/new_experiments/experiment_B/TimeDiT/weights/*.pt

# LS4 upstream-reproduction run outputs — 621 MiB of checkpoints, samples and
# TensorBoard events left over from the NN5/Monash reproduction runs used to
# validate the vendored LS4 code before the Heston work. They are EVIDENCE, kept
# on disk deliberately, but they must never be swept into a commit: a concurrent
# process stages this repo, and a stray `git add -A` would push 621 MiB of
# regenerable artefacts. Reproduce with code/reference/train.py on the matching
# configs/monash/*.yaml. The tracked reference/ tree (45 files: code, configs,
# LICENSE, README) is unaffected.
methods/LS4/code/reference/outputs_*

# Deep-MKV-TS upstream run trees — 357 MiB of per-seed checkpoints (6 x step_*.pt),
# per-checkpoint evaluation dumps (validation_bank.npy, 8192x128) and the
# validation-only hyperparameter-search trials. Every number we report is exported
# out of these trees into methods/Deep-MKV-TS/{generated_paths,weights,losses}/ and
# paper_reimplementation/results/, all of which ARE tracked. The raw trees stay on
# disk as evidence and are fully regenerable:
#   methods/Deep-MKV-TS/paper_reimplementation/metric/run_reproduction.sh   (seeds 0,1,3,4)
#   methods/Deep-MKV-TS/paper_reimplementation/metric/run_seed2.sh          (seed 2)
# runs/hpsearch* in particular backs NO reported number (validation split only).
methods/Deep-MKV-TS/paper_reimplementation/runs/

# HestonMultiAsset arrays — 126 MiB each, 1.26 GiB total, over GitHub's 100 MiB
# per-file hard limit and too large for the free LFS quota. The eight assets'
# parameters are frozen by PARAM_SEED=1234 and dumped to the TRACKED
# parameters.json, so the MODEL is always reproduced exactly. The Brownian noise
# is reproduced bitwise on a fixed numpy version, but np.random.Generator streams
# are NOT guaranteed stable across numpy versions (unlike legacy RandomState), so
# arrays regenerated under a different numpy are statistically -- not bitwise --
# identical. Reproduce in ~4 min on 8 cores:
#   cd dataset/HestonMultiAsset && python generate_heston_multiasset.py
dataset/HestonMultiAsset/*.npy

# Same reasoning for everything DERIVED from that d = 8 dataset: each generated
# array is (8192, 252, 8) float64 = 132 MB, again over GitHub's 100 MB per-file
# hard limit. Unlike the d = 1 arrays (8.4 MB each) these cannot be tracked.
# They are fully reproducible from tracked code + tracked hyperparameters:
#   SBTS paths       -> results/HestonMultiAsset/SBTS/code/run_all_multiasset.py
#                       (h, K, N_pi in losses/seed_i_bandwidth.json; per-worker
#                        RNG seeds derived deterministically from the run seed)
#   perfect-recovery -> metrics/gen_perfect_recovery_multiasset.py
#                       (independent draws at seed 1000+i, parameters frozen by
#                        PARAM_SEED=1234 and verified against parameters.json)
# The metadata.json next to each array IS tracked, so shapes, price ranges and
# generation times remain auditable without the payload.
#
# One pattern covers every method: the d = 8 tree is SELF-CONTAINED, i.e.
# results/HestonMultiAsset/<method>/ holds code/, generated_paths/, losses/,
# weights/ and the README side by side, instead of splitting inputs under
# methods/ and outputs under results/ the way the d = 1 benchmark does.
results/HestonMultiAsset/**/generated_paths/**/*.npy

# TrueDataset — the REAL d = 8 panel. Three separate reasons, all disqualifying:
#   raw/    27.7 GiB of Binance monthly 1s kline zips (536 files). Never push these;
#           they are a byte-for-byte copy of a public archive and are re-fetched,
#           checksum-verified, by `python download_binance.py`.
#   cache/  403 MiB of per-symbol-month parsed .npz (already covered by the global
#           `cache/` rule above; listed here so the reason is on the record).
#   *.npy   the ten split arrays plus five t0 index arrays, 64 MiB each, 640 MiB
#           total. Under GitHub's 100 MiB per-file cap individually, but LFS was
#           ruled out on 2026-07-30 and 640 MiB of regenerable payload has no
#           business in a git history.
# Unlike the Heston panel there is no PARAM_SEED to pin: the "model" is the public
# archive itself. The build is nonetheless DETERMINISTIC — the split layout comes
# from np.linspace over the window count with no RNG anywhere — so a rebuild from
# the same raw archives is bitwise identical. The TRACKED dataset_stats.json pins
# the exact build (panel, period, split date bounds, staleness and correlation
# diagnostics) so the arrays stay auditable without the payload. Reproduce:
#   cd dataset/TrueDataset && python download_binance.py \
#     && OMP_NUM_THREADS=1 taskset -c 0-11 python build_true_dataset.py --workers 12
dataset/TrueDataset/*.npy
dataset/TrueDataset/raw/
dataset/TrueDataset/logs/
# `variants/` is where the arrays actually live -- one subdirectory per build
# (period, N, split mode), each holding the ten split arrays. The bare
# `dataset/TrueDataset/*.npy` rule above does NOT reach them, so nine variants'
# worth of regenerable payload sat stageable. The per-variant dataset_stats.json
# is .json and stays tracked, which is what keeps the build auditable.
dataset/TrueDataset/variants/**/*.npy

# Same reasoning for everything DERIVED from the real panel. Each generated array
# is (8192, 128, 8) float64 = 67 MB. Reproducible from tracked code + tracked
# hyperparameters (h, K, N_pi in losses/bandwidth_selection.json). The tree is
# SELF-CONTAINED in the same way as results/HestonMultiAsset/.
results/TrueDataset/**/generated_paths/**/*.npy
# The published tree lives at results/trueexperiment/ (SBTS/ + real_floor/), and
# the same rule has to reach it: generated_paths/ under both, plus the CRPS banks
# at SBTS/crps_banks/generated_paths/. real_floor/generated_paths/ holds SYMLINKS
# into dataset/TrueDataset/variants/ -- ignoring them is deliberate, a tracked
# symlink pointing at an ignored file is a dangling reference on any clone.
results/trueexperiment/**/generated_paths/**/*.npy
